@@host: On the night of the twenty-fourth of August, in the year four hundred and ten, a gate in the northern wall of Rome swung open, from the inside.

@@host: For eight hundred years, no foreign army had set foot within the city. Rome had been sacked once before, by the Gauls, when it was still a town of farmers beside the Tiber. Since then it had become the capital of the known world. Its walls enclosed hundreds of thousands of people, the temples of a thousand years, and the idea, held by almost everyone alive, that Rome was eternal.

@@host: Far away, in a monastery at Bethlehem, an old scholar named Jerome heard the news, and wrote:

[pause 0.6]
@@jerome: My voice sticks in my throat; and as I dictate, sobs choke my utterance. The city which had taken the whole world, was itself taken.

[pause 2]

@@host: Thirteen and a half centuries later, an Englishman named Edward Gibbon set out to explain how it had come to this. His History of the Decline and Fall of the Roman Empire would fill six volumes, published over twelve years. This is his account of that night.

